fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- summary: The numbers match the recorded evidence, and the no-winner, qualification and scope caveats are handled correctly. Three wording and figure issues still assert things the evidence does not record: B3's construction, the non-vacuity threshold, and the \"additional search machinery\" framing.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `controls-nonvacuity-threshold-1` (Results/Controls (Figure 3 caption and figure image)): The evidence records only a boolean nonvacuous=true and the spread ratios (0.088, 0.160). It records no threshold. The manuscript says this itself (\"The evidence does not state the thresholds or construction details behind the non-vacuity indicator ... we make no claim about them\"). The caption and plot assert a specific evaluator limit that no artifact supports.\n  - claim: Figure 3b caption: \"with the evaluator's non-vacuity limit of 0.5\"; the plotted dashed line is labelled \"evaluator limit 0.5\".\n  - evidence: derived/final_stats.json permutation_controls (F5, F6 contain only bt_std_ratio, expected_moved_fraction, moved_fraction, nonvacuous); paper_data.json only contains 'nonvacuous' flags; scripts/build_figures.py line 91-92 hard-codes the 0.5 line; claims_grounding.json claim on controls lists no threshold; paper.tex Section 3.4 (method_comp) final sentences.\n  - requested_fix: Remove the 0.5 line and the phrase 'evaluator's non-vacuity limit of 0.5' from Figure 3b and its caption. Report only the recorded spread ratios and the evaluator's non-vacuous flag.\n- `b3-construction-claim-1` (Problem Setup and Method, 3.4 Comparators and validity controls): The same paragraph says the evaluator source is not staged and B1-B3 construction is not verified. This sentence nonetheless states what B3 is (hand-built, no gradient). The evidence trail records only B3's budgets and scores.\n  - claim: \"B3 is described by the evaluator only as an opponent-targeted removal, and we treat it as a hand-built comparator that needs no gradient.\"\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claim 'Evaluator source for comparators B1-B3 and the controls is not staged; only recorded outcomes ... are reported and construction details are not claimed'; derived/final_stats.json holds only b3_k, b3_percent and b3_score; paper.tex Section 3.4.\n  - requested_fix: Delete the clause 'we treat it as a hand-built comparator that needs no gradient'. Keep only that B3 is recorded by the evaluator as an opponent-targeted removal, with its recorded budgets.\n- `search-machinery-overreach-1` (Abstract; Introduction (What we find iii, Contributions 3)): This wording presents the B2 reference as the studied configuration minus its extra steps. The body says B2's construction is unverified, that B2 is not asserted to equal step (a), and that no conclusion about the re-linearisation, leverage correction or polish may be drawn. The abstract and introduction therefore overreach the grounded claim, which is only that the recorded budgets coincide with the evaluator's influence-only reference.\n  - claim: Abstract: \"these data show no measured gain from the additional search machinery\"; Introduction (iii): \"the held-out data show no measured improvement from the additional search machinery.\"\n  - evidence: paper.tex Sections 3.4 and 4.4 ('we do not conclude ... that the re-linearisation, leverage correction and polish contributed nothing'); claims_grounding.json claim 'B2's construction is unverified and no conclusion depends on it'; claims_grounding.json claim 'B2 is not asserted to equal any step of the studied configuration'.\n  - requested_fix: Rephrase both places to say the studied configuration's recorded budgets equal the evaluator's recorded influence-only reference budgets in all six held-out cases, so no measured reduction relative to that reference is observed. Drop the reference to the 'additional search machinery'.\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. Three targeted retrieval targets.\n\nCoverage of Bradley–Terry theory (extensions, existence/consistency, uncertainty) is dense. The existing bibliography does not cover three areas the draft relies on or motivates itself with. The draft concedes this in its \"Scope of the bibliography\" paragraph and in the Extended limitations. Numeric claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not targets.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Chatbot Arena / MT-Bench pairwise LLM evaluation and the datasets used\n- Influence functions and leave-one-out / Cook's-distance-style deletion diagnostics\n- Manipulation and rigging of crowd-vote or pairwise-comparison rankings\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **arena-leaderboard-context** (Introduction, high, benchmark_context)\n   - Anchor: \"Leaderboards that aggregate 'which of A and B is better' judgments into Bradley–Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models…\"\n   - Reason: The motivating claim about leaderboard use has no citation. The Data section uses Arena 55k/140k and MT-Bench human judgments without citing the platform or dataset papers.\n   - Search topics: Chatbot Arena platform paper; MT-Bench human judgments / LLM-as-a-judge; Arena human-preference dataset.\n\n2. **influence-deletion-method** (Problem Setup and Method, high, background)\n   - Anchor: \"Deleting row $i$ perturbs the stationarity condition by $-\\nabla\\ell_i$, so a Newton step predicts the change of the gap…\"\n   - Reason: The first-order influence formula with leverage correction is the core of the studied configuration and the basis of the B2 comparator. It has no citation to influence-function or case-deletion literature. Only Broderick et al. is cited.\n   - Search topics: influence functions for black-box predictions; Cook's distance / case deletion in logistic regression; one-step leave-one-out approximations for M-estimators.\n\n3. **manipulation-positioning** (Related Work, medium, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"Work on adversarial manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards, on evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on influence functions…\"\n   - Reason: The draft motivates the removal budget through deliberate action and rigging, then disclaims any positioning against that work. One or two citations would replace the disclaimer.\n   - Search topics: adversarial vote manipulation of LLM arena leaderboards; robustness of pairwise-comparison rankings to corrupted comparisons.\n\n## Not targeted\nRelated-work paragraphs on BT extensions, estimation and uncertainty are well supported. Adding more there would only grow the bibliography.\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261111_075327.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_075327.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_reference_retrieval.log\npostdraft_references.bib\npostdraft_references_provenance.json\n6479 paper.tex\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
