{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WA1jJSEAuSe4MYv58Tgwdz","type":"tool_result","content":"43:Leaderboards that aggregate\n80:Deleting row $i$ perturbs t\n43 Leaderboards that aggregate ``which of A and B is better'' judgments into Bradley--Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models, to announce releases, and to compare research systems; Chatbot Arena is a prominent crowd-vote example \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}. The BT model and its many extensions are decades old \\citep{Davidson1970OnET,Davidson1969MultivariatePC,Leonard1977AnAB}, and the statistical properties of its estimates under sampling noise are increasingly well understood \\citep{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT,Gao2021UncertaintyQI,Li2021ThePO}. What is less often reported is a complementary, \\emph{combinatorial} question: how much of the underlying comparison table has to change, whether through noise or deliberate action, before a published rank order reverses?\n\n80 Deleting row $i$ perturbs the stationarity condition by $-\\nabla\\ell_i$, so a Newton step predicts the change of the gap $g=\\theta_{\\hi}-\\theta_{\\lo}$ as, in the manner of influence-function analyses of the effect of removing a training point \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730},\n\n321 \\subsection{Extended limitations}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"075e3a46-b5c4-405b-bb4b-6ee37a2af972","uuid":"118cf81b-b061-4e46-a531-53fdeeae526c","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:38:20.724Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"43:Leaderboards that aggregate\n80:Deleting row $i$ perturbs t\n43 Leaderboards that aggregate ``which of A and B is better'' judgments into Bradley--Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models, to announce releases, and to compare research systems; Chatbot Arena is a prominent crowd-vote example \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132}. The BT model and its many extensions are decades old \\citep{Davidson1970OnET,Davidson1969MultivariatePC,Leonard1977AnAB}, and the statistical properties of its estimates under sampling noise are increasingly well understood \\citep{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT,Gao2021UncertaintyQI,Li2021ThePO}. What is less often reported is a complementary, \\emph{combinatorial} question: how much of the underlying comparison table has to change, whether through noise or deliberate action, before a published rank order reverses?\n\n80 Deleting row $i$ perturbs the stationarity condition by $-\\nabla\\ell_i$, so a Newton step predicts the change of the gap $g=\\theta_{\\hi}-\\theta_{\\lo}$ as, in the manner of influence-function analyses of the effect of removing a training point \\citep{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730},\n\n321 \\subsection{Extended limitations}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
